  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nDevelopment of mid to upper riding to the northeast of the \nforecast area into the Central Great Plains over time early \nthrough the middle of next week is still expected. This will allow\nfor increased southerly return flow and increased near surface \nmoisture, as well as the area being under the western/southern \n\"ring of fire\" of passing disturbances overhead providing moisture\nconvergence and lift aloft interacting with humid, upslope \nsoutheast winds east of lee troughing from central NM into \nwesternmost TX. Highs will not change much from day to day, \nalthough warmest daytime temperatures above 100F can still be \nexpected along the Pecos River valley, over the northeast Permian \nBasin, and along the Rio Grande, with 105F-110F readings each day \nfor the Big Bend. Everywhere else is forecast to remain in the \n90F-100F range, but everyone is still encouraged to take proper \nheat safety precautions if outdoors or in vehicles. Low (15% to \n25%) afternoon/evening shower/storm chances are apparent over the \nwestern higher terrain into SE NM plains. At this time, further \ninformation regarding timing and coverage is not clear, but no \nmajor flooding or severe weather is indicated. Lows remain similar\nto the short term with no real pattern change in sight offering \ncooler and/or more rainy weather as we head into early September.",
